This is Ghostty's config, translated from the iTerm2 profile in ../iterm2
(kept there for reference/comparison; its cask is commented out in
../homebrew/Brewfile now that ghostty has replaced it).

Ghostty reads its config from ~/.config/ghostty/config (there's no prefs
folder setting like iTerm's PrefsCustomFolder), so it lives here at
.config/ghostty/config.symlink - sub_bootstrap in ../bin/dotfiles mirrors
a topic dir's *.symlink files onto $HOME as-is (minus the .symlink
suffix), so this one lands at ~/.config/ghostty/config. Run
`dotfiles bootstrap` (or just `ln -sf` it yourself) to pick up changes.

Settings from the iTerm profile with no direct Ghostty equivalent (i.e.
not translated into .config/ghostty/config.symlink):

- DimBackgroundWindows: iTerm dims whole unfocused *windows*, not just
  split panes. Ghostty only has unfocused-split-opacity (panes within one
  window); nothing dims an unfocused window.
- DimOnlyText: iTerm can dim just the text of inactive panes, leaving the
  background alone. Ghostty's unfocused-split-opacity dims the whole pane.
- Disable Window Resizing: prevented apps/escape sequences from resizing
  the iTerm window. No Ghostty equivalent found; likely less necessary
  since Ghostty's resize is instant rather than jarring.
- AlternateMouseScroll: had scroll wheel send arrow keys in the alt screen
  (e.g. less, vim). This is standard/default behavior in most modern
  terminals including Ghostty, so no setting should be needed - verify.
- Custom Keyboard Map: iTerm had explicit remaps for Option/Cmd + Arrow/
  Delete to send readline-style word/line-editing sequences (word-left/
  right, delete-word, kill-line, etc). Ghostty ships its own macOS-native
  defaults for these same combos, so this probably doesn't need
  reproducing - but test them after switching, and add `keybind` lines in
  ./config if anything regresses.
- Per-pane/window chrome: ShowPaneTitles, WindowNumber, UseBorder,
  TabStyleWithAutomaticOption, TabViewType, PreserveWindowSizeWhenTabBar-
  VisibilityChanges. These are iTerm-specific UI chrome with no Ghostty
  config equivalent - Ghostty uses native macOS tabs/window chrome
  instead.

One thing worth double-checking given how much SSH shows up in the iTerm
profile (Jobs to Ignore: ssh, rlogin, slogin, telnet): Ghostty reports
TERM=xterm-ghostty by default, not xterm-256color like iTerm. Most modern
remote hosts handle this fine, but older ones may not have the
xterm-ghostty terminfo entry installed, which can break things like
tmux/vim on that host. Install Ghostty's terminfo there with:

    infocmp -x xterm-ghostty | ssh <host> -- tic -x -

or set `term = xterm-256color` in .config/ghostty/config.symlink to
sidestep it entirely.
